---
/**
 * Accessibility statement.
 *
 * Structured on the W3C model statement — conformance, measures, technical
 * specifications, assessment, known limitations, feedback, review — so that it
 * can be read against any other organisation's and compared section for
 * section.
 *
 * Every claim in section 3 was measured against the built site rather than
 * asserted. If you change the site in a way that breaks one, change the
 * sentence too: a statement that has drifted from the site is worse than none.
 *
 * The measurements: every page carries a language and exactly one first-level
 * heading with no level skipped; every form control resolves to a label; every
 * focusable element shows a focus ring; no text falls below its WCAG AA
 * contrast threshold; every image carries alt text; every page renders in full
 * with scripting off apart from the account page; no page scrolls sideways
 * between 390 and 2560 wide.
 */
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';

const LAST_REVIEWED = '9 September 2026';
---

<Doc
	title="Accessibility Statement — The Itti Foundation"
	description="The accessibility of itti.org.in: conformance status, measures taken, how it was assessed, known limitations, and how to report a problem."
	eyebrow="Accessibility"
	heading="Accessibility Statement"
	lede="Our commitment, what conformance we claim, how it was assessed, and what we know is still wrong."
	updated={LAST_REVIEWED}
>
	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">1. Our commitment</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				The Itti Foundation is committed to making <code>itti.org.in</code> usable by as
				many people as possible, regardless of disability, device or circumstance.
			</p>
			<p>
				One of the three areas this Foundation works in is neurodivergence. A site that is
				hard to read, that cannot be operated without a mouse, or that moves when someone
				has asked it not to, would contradict its own purpose.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">2. Conformance status</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				We target <strong
					>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2, Level AA</strong
				>.
			</p>
			<p>
				This site is <strong>partially conformant</strong> with WCAG 2.2 Level AA:
				most of the standard is met, and the exceptions are listed in section 6. We do not
				claim certification, and the assessment has not been independently verified.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">3. Measures taken</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				Each of the following was verified against the built site, on every page, at
				viewport widths from a 390-pixel phone to a 2560-pixel display.
			</p>
			<dl class="doc__list">
				<dt>Contrast</dt>
				<dd>
					No text falls below its Level AA threshold: 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for
					large text. Three of our four accent colours failed as small type and have been
					darkened for that use; they are unchanged as rules and marks.
				</dd>
				<dt>Keyboard operation</dt>
				<dd>
					Every interactive element is reachable and operable by keyboard and displays a
					visible focus indicator. A skip link is the first focusable element on every
					page.
				</dd>
				<dt>Structure and semantics</dt>
				<dd>
					Every page declares its language, carries exactly one first-level heading,
					skips no heading level, and identifies its banner, navigation, main content and
					footer as landmarks.
				</dd>
				<dt>Forms</dt>
				<dd>
					All twenty-eight controls on the registration form have programmatically
					associated labels. Errors are conveyed in text and announced to assistive
					technology, not by colour alone.
				</dd>
				<dt>Images</dt>
				<dd>
					Every image carries alternative text. Artwork in the opening sequence is marked
					decorative, because the adjacent text carries its meaning.
				</dd>
				<dt>Motion</dt>
				<dd>
					Where the operating system requests reduced motion, the opening sequence stops
					animating: no camera movement, no dissolve, no drift. The three images still
					change, so no content is lost; they cut rather than fade.
				</dd>
				<dt>Reflow and zoom</dt>
				<dd>
					No page requires horizontal scrolling at any width in the range tested, and
					content reflows to a single column on small viewports.
				</dd>
				<dt>Operation without scripting</dt>
				<dd>
					Every page renders in full with JavaScript disabled, with one exception noted
					in section 6.
				</dd>
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">4. Technical specifications</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				Accessibility relies on HTML, CSS, WAI-ARIA and JavaScript, and on the
				combination of a modern browser with any assistive technology installed on the
				visitor&rsquo;s device.
			</p>
			<p>
				The site is served as static pages over HTTPS. Typefaces are self-hosted, and no
				third-party script runs on any page.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">5. Assessment approach</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				The Foundation assessed this site by self-evaluation. Testing combined automated
				checks of the built output with manual keyboard operation, contrast measurement
				against rendered composites rather than nominal colours, and inspection with
				scripting disabled and with reduced motion requested.
			</p>
			<p>
				Self-evaluation is the weakest form of assessment available, and section 6 says so
				plainly.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">6. Known limitations</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				Despite our efforts, the following are known to fall short. Each is ours to fix.
			</p>
			<dl class="doc__list">
				<dt>No assistive-technology user testing</dt>
				<dd>
					The structure is correct by measurement, which is not the same as being good to
					listen to. We have not yet tested with anyone who uses a screen reader daily.
				</dd>
				<dt>No independent audit</dt>
				<dd>
					Every claim on this page was verified by the people who built the site.
				</dd>
				<dt>The account page requires scripting</dt>
				<dd>
					It retrieves your record from our registry as it opens and cannot do so with
					JavaScript disabled. It states this, and gives an address at which a person will
					provide the same information.
				</dd>
				<dt>The opening sequence is animated</dt>
				<dd>
					It is the most demanding element on the site. Reduced motion is the alternative
					we offer; there is no static version of the page.
				</dd>
				<dt>Some navigation leads to unwritten pages</dt>
				<dd>
					Much of the site is not yet written. Those links reach a page that says so,
					which is honest but remains a dead end.
				</dd>
				<dt>English only</dt>
				<dd>
					We ask your preferred language at registration and cannot yet correspond in it.
				</dd>
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">7. Feedback</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				We welcome reports of accessibility barriers. You do not need to know what the
				problem is called or which guideline it falls under &mdash; &ldquo;I could not get
				past this part&rdquo; is a useful report.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Email:</strong>{' '}
				<a class="doc__link" href="mailto:hello@itti.org.in">hello@itti.org.in</a><br />
				<strong>Telephone:</strong>{' '}
				<a class="doc__link" href="tel:+918928263631">+91 89282 63631</a>
			</p>
			<p>
				We aim to respond within five working days. If anything on this site prevents you
				from registering, tell us and we will take your details by whatever means is
				easiest for you.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">8. Review</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				This statement was last reviewed on {LAST_REVIEWED}. It is reviewed whenever the
				site changes materially, and at least once a year.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Doc>
